messages:
lucas: FYI the android sample using coroutines + retrofit is occasionally duplicating uploads after a token refresh. repro on API 30 and 33.
maria: dupes how? double POST or same request retried?
lucas: double POST. flow: suspend interceptor runs, calls authApi.refreshToken() (suspend), then proceeds to re-execute the original call. server receives two bodies for the same payload id.
anil: are you using an OkHttp interceptor or a Retrofit CallAdapter that does suspend conversions? mixing suspend in an interceptor can be tricky.
lucas: Interceptor (we needed to inspect request metadata). code excerpt:

	class SuspendAuthInterceptor(private val provider: TokenProvider): Interceptor {
	  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
	    val token = provider.getValidTokenSuspend() // calls suspend function via runBlocking here
	    val req = chain.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer $token").build()
	    return chain.proceed(req)
	  }
	}

lucs: oops that is runBlocking inside interceptor. can cause dispatcher/thread pool starvation and reentrancy.
kevin: runBlocking inside interceptors is a red flag. Also multipart bodies (streaming RequestBody) aren't reusable: if the original call gets buffered vs retried you can end up re-sending same stream unexpectedly.
sam: how many threads in the OkHttp Dispatcher? if you block the call thread waiting for refresh, other queued calls might start and also block, leading to multiple simultaneous refresh attempts.
lucas: Dispatcher is default. we saw logs: 
	I/OkHttp: --> POST /v1/upload (Content-Type: multipart/form-data)
	I/Auth: token expired, refreshing (runBlocking)
	I/OkHttp: --> POST /v1/upload (Authorization: Bearer new)
	W/Server: duplicate upload id 0x7f1 - rejected

maria: two practical options: 1) use an Authenticator which OkHttp calls on 401 and returns a new request (avoids nesting network calls inside interceptor), or 2) have the interceptor use a non-blocking refresh that uses a separate client or a single-thread refresh queue.
anil: for coroutines we prefer to keep the interceptor synchronous and have TokenProvider expose a non-blocking getToken() that returns cached token or a Deferred you await outside the interceptor. But because interceptors are synchronous we used runBlocking which is causing the problem.
kevin: another important point: if you retry the same call by calling chain.proceed() again after refresh you must ensure request bodies are replayable. For streaming bodies use a buffering layer or upload ids so server can dedupe idempotent operations.
sam: customer DX note: sample app used retrofit.create(AuthApi::class.java) with the same retrofit instance. if you need to refresh from the network inside auth logic, create an authRetrofit with its own OkHttpClient (small dispatcher, no interceptors) to avoid reentrancy.
lucas: i implemented an authClient with a single-thread dispatcher and switched provider to use it. also replaced runBlocking with a suspend-friendly approach: the interceptor now checks provider.tokenState atomically and, if a refresh is already in-flight, waits on a CompletableDeferred that the refresher completes.
maria: can you paste the token provider pattern? that would be a great sample for devex.
lucas: simplified snippet:

	class TokenProvider {
	  @Volatile private var token = ""
	  @Volatile private var refreshing: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null

	  suspend fun getValidToken(): String {
	    if (!isExpired(token)) return token
	    val current = synchronized(this) {
	      refreshing ?: run {
	        val d = CompletableDeferred<String>()
	        refreshing = d
	        launchRefreshInAuthClient(d) // starts coroutine that calls authRetrofit and completes d
	        d
	      }
	    }
	    return current.await()
	  }
	}

lucas: with that we only run one network refresh and all waiters get the new token. cleared dupes in local.
kevin: please add a short test (Robolectric or instrumented) that exercises two concurrent uploads with forced expiry to verify no duplicates. Also document that interceptors should not perform network calls on the same client.
sam: agree — i'll add a small sample under devex/samples/android/auth-refresh-coroutines.md and a minimal code sample app demonstrating authClient + TokenProvider.
ci-bot: created ticket DEVEX-3121: "android: coroutine-friendly token provider + auth client sample" and queued for sprint.
lucas: PR incoming with changes + README note: "Don't call your main Retrofit/OkHttp client to refresh tokens from inside an interceptor; prefer OkHttp.Authenticator, a dedicated auth client, or a coordinated token provider."
maria: awesome. also add a note about multipart streaming: prefer idempotency keys for uploads so server dedupes safely.
lucas: will include tests and a migration note for Android API differences. thanks everyone :+1:
